pyEPW (formerly open-meteo-epw) is an independent community library.
The installable distribution is named pyepw-openmeteo; the Python import is
pyEPW. This project is independent of the unrelated pyepw project on PyPI.
It is not an official
Open-Meteo, ECMWF, Copernicus, GeoNames, or EnergyPlus product.

The MIT licence covers this library's code and documentation. External data
and service access retain their separate licences and terms.

Weather: Open-Meteo (https://open-meteo.com/), ERA5/ERA5-Land from Copernicus
Climate Change Service / ECMWF. Credits and modifications are in output metadata.
Data licence: https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/
Hosted API terms: https://open-meteo.com/en/terms
The public hosted API is restricted to non-commercial use and rate limited.

Optional city catalog: GeoNames cities500, Creative Commons Attribution 4.0.
https://download.geonames.org/export/dump/readme.txt
It is downloaded separately on demand and is not bundled in the package.

Optional station supplementation: NOAA NCEI Global Historical Climatology
Network-hourly (GHCNh), with credit to its originating observing networks.
https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/global-historical-climatology-network-hourly
Station metadata, observations and source/QC codes are downloaded separately.
Nearby observations are spatial proxies; no on-site measurement is implied.

Additional gap-only station sources: NOAA NCEI Integrated Surface Database (ISD)
and original published METAR reports archived by Iowa Environmental Mesonet.
https://www.ncei.noaa.gov/products/land-based-station/integrated-surface-database
https://mesonet.agron.iastate.edu/request/download.phtml
IEM describes very little additional archive QC; these records are labelled
separately from QC-passed NOAA records. Sources are fetched on demand.

Surface albedo: NASA POWER, derived from CERES SYN1deg. Original source headers,
parameter, requested location, UTC timestamps and valid-data coverage are kept.
https://power.larc.nasa.gov/docs/methodology/energy-fluxes/derived-parameters/
Undefined/provider-missing hourly ratios are not replaced with invented values.

Daylight model: Perez, R., Ineichen, P., Seals, R., Michalsky, J. and Stewart, R.
(1990), Modeling daylight availability and irradiance components from direct
and global irradiance, Solar Energy 44(5), 271–289.
https://doi.org/10.1016/0038-092X(90)90055-H
Independently implemented using the paper's equations and coefficient Table 4;
no third-party daylight implementation is bundled. Kasten–Young (1989) relative
air mass is used as a documented adaptation. Outputs are empirical estimates.

Solar geometry: independent implementation of NOAA's published Meeus-based
geometric solar equations; Spencer (1971) Earth-distance harmonic correction,
with a 1361 W/m² solar constant.
https://gml.noaa.gov/grad/solcalc/calcdetails.html
